          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes is a condition where your body's tissues do not respond well to insulin, a hormone that regulates blood sugar. This is called insulin resistance. Because of this resistance, your pancreas has to work harder to produce more insulin. Eventually, it cannot produce enough to keep your blood glucose (sugar) levels in the normal range. The result is that your blood glucose levels stay elevated.\n\nThis condition develops gradually over several years. There's often a period before diagnosis called prediabetes, where blood glucose levels are raised but not yet in the diabetic range. Intensive lifestyle changes during this prediabetes stage can have a significant effect.\n\nThis information comes from a general clinical reference educational summary titled \"Type 2 diabetes mellitus.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
