          "question": "And what should I be eating?",
          "answer": "For type 2 diabetes, a healthy eating pattern is crucial. A dietary approach that emphasizes vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts, and vegetable oils is often recommended. It's also advised to limit saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets, and sugar-sweetened drinks. This comprehensive pattern is more effective than focusing on single foods.\n\nIf your body weight is above a healthy range, reducing it can improve glucose control, blood pressure, and lipid levels. Even a sustained reduction of five percent can show measurable benefits. It's important to remember that \"sugar-free\" does not mean \"carbohydrate-free,\" as starches can also raise blood glucose. Whole fruit is generally considered part of a recommended eating pattern.\n\nThis information comes from the \"Diet for type 2 diabetes\" and \"Diet for high blood pressure\" educational summaries.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet, lifestyle-hypertension-diet]",
